1
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:15,671
June on the fast sea ice of Atka Bay, near Neumayer Station III in Antarctica. You stand at one point one meters — emperor height.

2
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:24,565
Look up.

3
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:47,774
Green oxygen emissions of the aurora australis sweep the polar night. Now look down. You are not alone.

4
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:14,590
Thousands of male emperor penguins, Aptenodytes forsteri, press together on frozen ocean in minus forty-degree wind.

5
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:35,726
Each male balances a single egg on his tarsometatarsal feet beneath a vascularized abdominal brood pouch.

6
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:59,742
Notice how the colony flows past our stationary mount. Biophysicist Daniel Zitterbart filmed this exact Atka Bay colony.

7
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:21,651
Every thirty to sixty seconds, tiny five-to-ten-centimeter steps propagate as a traveling wave, cycling outer birds inward.

8
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:42,867
Listen to your left and astern. Individual display calls cut cleanly through katabatic wind and thousands of bodies.

9
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:05,598
Because emperors build no fixed nest, Thierry Aubin and Pierre Jouventin showed they rely on a two-voice syrinx signature.

10
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:27,853
Two simultaneous fundamental frequencies generate acoustic beats unique to each individual, allowing mates to reunite in darkness.

11
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:52,911
Look straight ahead to the northern horizon. After weeks of continuous polar night, faint civil twilight touches the ice.

12
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:14,226
Incubating males fast for roughly one hundred and fifteen days, losing up to forty percent of their body mass.

13
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,072
This first glow signals mid-winter hatching — and the imminent return of foraging females from open pack-ice polynyas.

14
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:00,966
No other vertebrate breeds exposed on Antarctic sea ice through the depths of winter.

15
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:22,882
Observe wild Southern Ocean penguins live today on our Antipodes Island and Ōamaru penguin cameras at Digital Live Zoo.
